-- ==================== CHAPTER 11 ====================

-- Chapter 11: Indian Contract Act, 1872
insert into public.mcq_questions (book_id, chapter_no, chapter_title, question_no, question, options, correct_index, hint)
select b.id, 11, 'Indian Contract Act, 1872', v.question_no, v.question, v.options, v.correct_index, v.hint
from public.books b
cross join lateral (values
(1, 'In the case of rights to sell the goods of Pawnee, if the Pawnor fails to pay the money due under the pledge, and the Pawnee sells the good. If proceeds of sale is greater than amount due, what will be the effect of such sale?', '["The Pawnee will retain the money","The Pawnee will retain the half of the money","The Pawnee shall pay the surplus to the Pawnor","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'According to Section 176, if the Pawnor has failed to pay the debt or perform his promise, and the Pawnee has given a reasonable notice to the Pawnor of his intention to sell the goods. Then, if the proceeds of sale < Amount due: The Pawnee may recover the deficit from Pawnor, if the Proceeds of sale > Amount Due: The Pawnee shall pay the surplus to the Pawnor.'),
(2, 'A gives authority to B to sell A''s land, and to pay himself, out of the proceeds, the debts due to him from A. Decide whether A can revoke the authority of B?', '["A cannot revoke","A can revoke","A can revoke to some extent","Can''t Say"]'::jsonb, 0, 'According to section 202, it is the case of agency coupled with interest and hereunder, A cannot revoke the authority of B. Also, the agency cannot be terminated even in case of insanity or death of A.'),
(3, 'K is the wife of A. She purchased a saree on credit from B. B demanded the amount from A. A refused to make the payment. B filed a suit against A for the same amount. Decide in the light of provisions of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, whether B would succeed or not?', '["B is entitled to sue A","B Is entitled to sue K","B is not entitled to sue A & K","Can''t Say"]'::jsonb, 0, 'B is entitled to sue A, since a husband is liable for the necessities supplied to his wife and the wife has an implied authority to pledge his husband''s credit for necessities and hence it is a legal presumption that a married woman is an agent of her husband.'),
(4, 'What is the meaning of ''agent'' as per section 182?', '["A person employed to do any act for another","A person employed to represent another in dealing with third person","Either a or b","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Accor ding to Section 182 under the contract of agency, an agent Is a person employed to do any act for another; or represent another in dealings with third person.'),
(5, 'When the Pawnee can retain the pledged goods?', '["When the promise is not performed","Interest on debt not paid","Necessary expenses for preservation of Goods","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'According to section 173, rights of Lien to Pawnee until the Pawnor has money due unde r the pledge (or until the promise Is performed), Interest on debt not paid, and necessary expenses for preservation of goods.'),
(6, 'R of New Delhi sends his agent M to purchase certain goods from Global Enterprise, Mumbai on credit for him. Later on R pays the amount for the goods purchased. On another occasion the agent sends M to purchase goods but this time pays sufficient cash to M for the purpose. M, however again purchases the goods from Global Enterprise but on credit and soon thereafter he dies. Global Enterprise files a suit against R for recovery of the said amount. Decide whether Global Enterprise would be given any relief by the court under the provisions of the Indian Contract Act, 1872?', '["R is not liable to Global Enterprise","MÃ¢â‚¬â„¢s legal heir is liable to Global Enterprise","R is liable to Global Enterprise","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'R will be liable to Global Enterprise for goods purchased by M, since the principal is bound to third parties for all s uch acts of the agent as are within the scope of authority of the agent as a previous occasion, purchase of goods by M on behalf of R. and subsequent payment for such goods by R, established that it was within the scope of authority of M to purchase goods on credit on behalf of R.'),
(7, 'A hires a carriage of B and agrees to pay Rs. 500 as hire charges. The carriage is unsafe, though B is unaware of it A is injured and claims compensation for the injuries suffered by him. B refuses to pay. Discuss the liability of B?', '["B is not liable to pay the compensation","B is liable to pay the compensation","B is liable to pay half of the compensation of the damage so caused","B Is not liable to pay the compensation up to some extent"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Since the hire of carriage of B by an amounts to non - gratuitous benefit and It''s the duty of B to disclose to A that the carriage was unsafe (even though he was unaware about It), and hence B didnÃ¢â‚¬â„¢t disclose the same to A. Thus, B is liable to compensate for the injuries suffered by B due to non - disclosure of faults In the carriage.'),
(8, 'During which circumstances Bailee shall not be liable for any loss or destruction of goods?', '["He Is not negligent","The loss was caused due to an act of god or other unavoidable reasons","Both a or b","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'According to Section 152 of the Indian Contract Act 1872, the Bailee shall not be liable for any loss or destruction of goods, if he is not negligent, or the loss was caused due to an act of God or other unavoidable reasons.'),
(9, 'P appoints Q, a minor to sell his car for not less than Rs. 2.5Iacs. Q sells it for Rs. 2Iacs, whether P will be bound by the transaction or the transaction will be revoked?', '["P will be bound to sell the car for Rs. 2lacs","P will be liable to compensate the party involved, in case of revocation","P will not be bounded by the transaction","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 0, 'P will be bound to sell the car for Rs. 2lacs and Q, the minor will not be liable to P, as according to Section 184, an agent is not liable to the principal if he is a minor or is of unsound mind.'),
(10, 'The Delivery of goods by one person to another as security for the payment of a debt is called Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.?', '["Bailment","Pledge","Mortgage","Hypothecation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'According to Section 172, the bailment of goods as a security for payment of a debt or performance of a promise is called pledge.'),
(11, 'A delivers a rough diamond to B, a jeweler, to be cut and polished, which is accordingly done. Does B has particular lien for his liability if not been paid by A with context on contract of Bailment?', '["B Is entitled to retain the Diamond","B is not entitled to retain the Diamond","A can regain its possession back, only any part payment made thereon","Can''t say"]'::jsonb, 0, 'According to Section 170 of the Indian Contract Act, 187 2, B is entitled to retain the diamond till he is paid for the service he has rendered (B has particular Lien).'),
(12, 'Do the seizure of goods by custom authorities constitute a contract of bailment under the provisions of the Indian Contract Act, 1872?', '["No","Yes","Partly yes and Partly no","CanÃ¢â‚¬â„¢t say"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Yes, it result in a valid contract of bailment, as all the essentials of a valid contract of bailment are satisfied in such a case.'),
(15, 'What are the duties of an agent amongst the following?', '["To conduct the business In accordance with the directions given by the principal","Not to make any secret profit out of the agency business other than the agreed remuneration","No to use Information obtained In the course of the agency against principal","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'According to Section - 209 to 218, as per the contract of agency under the Indian Contract Act, 1872, there are various other duties along with the above duties are given thereon.'),
(16, 'What are the rights of an agent amongst the following?', '["To retain money out of the sums received In agency business for advances made or expenses Incurred and remuneration due to him","To claim compensation for injury caused to agent because of principal''s neglect or want of skill","Right of Lien on principal''s goods, papers and other property until the remuneration due to him is paid","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Refer to section 217 and 220 to 225, for the various other Rights of an agent, as per given in the Indian Contract Act, 1872.'),
(20, 'Which among the following can become an agent?', '["A minor","A person of unsound mind","Neither a nor b","Both a or b"]'::jsonb, 3, 'According to Section 184 under the contract of agency, any person may become an agent, even a minor or a person of unsound minor can become an agent.'),
(21, 'Mercantile Agent means an agent having the authority toÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦..?', '["Perform a particular act","Do all acts connected with a particular business of the principal","Act under the control of the original agent","Consign the goods for the purpose of sale"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Mercantile agent means an agent having the authority to sell the goods, consign the goods for the purchase of sale, buy the goods, raise money on the security of the goods, acting as agent in the ordinary course of business.'),
(22, 'Mr. A of Alwar engaged Mr. S as his agent to buy a house. Mr. S bought a house for Rs. 40 Lakhs in the name of a nominee and then purchased it himself for Rs. 44 Lakhs. He then sold the same house to Mr. A for Rs. 46 Lakhs. Mr. A later comes to know about the mischief of Mr. S and tries to recover the excess amount paid to Mr. S. Is he entitled to recover any amount from Mr. S and by how much?', '["Mr. A is not entitled to recover any amount from Mr. S","Mr. A Is entitled to repudiate the contract or to claim from Mr. S Rs. 6 lakhs","Mr. A is entitled to recover Rs. 4 lakhs from Mr. S","Mr. A Is entitled to repudiate the contract or to claim from Mr. S Rs. 2 Lakhs"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Non - disclosure of profit of Rs. 6 Lakhs amounts to br each of duty by Mr. S, since Mr. S without disclosing all the material facts and without obtaining the consent of Mr. A, dealt in the business of agency on his own account and Mr. S made a secret profit, so Mr. A is entitled to repudiate the contract or to claim from Mr. S Rs. 6 Lakhs.'),
(23, 'An agency in which the agent himself has interest in the subject matter of agency is calledÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.?', '["Agency by estoppel","Agency by Holding Out","Agency by necessity","Agency coupled with interest"]'::jsonb, 3, 'As per Section 202, when an agency is created for securing some benefit to the agent over and above his remuneration as an agent, it is called as agency coupled with Interest.'),
(24, 'When an agent is considered as Agency by Estoppel?', '["Law treating one person as an agent of another","A person who makes a representation to a third person that a certain person is his agent","A person by his affirmative conduct lead third person to believe that doing some act on his behalf Is doing with authority.","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, 'According to Section 237 of t he contract of agency under the Indian Contract Act, 1872, an agency by estoppel is considered, if a person makes a representation to a third person that a certain person is his agent, and the third party, believing such representation to be true, enters i nto a contract with the pretended agent.'),
(25, 'Which among the following is to be considered as a Gratuitous Bailment?', '["For the exclusive benefit of Bailor","For the exclusive benefit of Bailee","For the mutual benefit of Bailor & Bailee","Both a or b"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Gratuitous Bailment is defined as the bailment without any charges or reward, i.e., no hire charges are be paid by Bailee and no custody charges are paid by Bailor.'),
(26, 'Define the term ''PAWNEE'' under the contract of Pledge?', '["The Bailor in case of a pledge","The Bailee in case of a pledge","The third party in case of a pledge","Related Party of a Bailor"]'::jsonb, 1, 'According to Section 172, the Bailor in case of pledge is called as Pawnor and the Bailee in case of pledge is called as Pawnee.'),
(27, 'When the goods are mixed without Bailor''s consent and goods are not separable, then what will be the consequences of the same?', '["The parties will have a proportionate interest in such mixture","The Bailee shall pay the expenses of damage of separation","The Bailee is not liable for it","The Bailee shall be liable to pay damages to the Bailor for any loss caused to him"]'::jsonb, 3, 'According to Section 157 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, the Bailee shall be liable to pay damages to the Bailor for any loss caused to him as t he goods are mixed without Bailor''s consent and goods are not separable.'),
(28, 'When the Bailee has the right to be indemnified by the Bailor?', '["When the Bailor has no title to the goods.","When the Bailee suffers any loss, caused because of Bailor","Both a and b","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'According to Section 166, the Bailee has the right to be Indemnified by the Bailor, If the Bailor has no title to the goods, and as a consequence, the Bailee suffers from lo ss.'),
(29, 'A (n)Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦. is a contract a principal and agent enter into that says the principal cannot employ another agent other than the one stated.', '["Ratified Contract","Exclusive Agency Contract","Apparent agency contract","Exclusive principle contract"]'::jsonb, 1, 'If principal and an agent enter into an exclusive agency contract, the principal cannot employ any agent other than the exclusive agent.'),
(32, 'Z guarantees to Y, to the extent of Rs. 2,000, payment for rice to be supplied by Y to X. Y supplies to X rice amounting to Rs. 500, but obtains from Z payment of the sum of Rs. 2000 in respect of the rice supplied. Decide whether Y can recover the amount from Z?', '["Y can recover from Z amounting Rs. 2,000","Y cannot recover from Z, more than Rs. 500","Y can recover from Z Rs. 500 and balance amount from X","Y can recover from Z Rs. 1500 and balance amount from X"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Y cannot recover fro m Z more than Rs. 500 as the market value of the amount actually supplied is tenable and cannot earn excess profit from it, without the consent of Z.'),
(33, 'S and P go into a shop. S says to the shopkeeper, C, "Let P have the goods, and if he does not pay you, I will. This is a Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦', '["Contract of Guarantee","Contract of Indemnity","Wagering agreement","Quasi-contract"]'::jsonb, 0, NULL),
(34, 'What are the reason for loss that is to be considered under the contract of indemnity u/s 24?', '["Conduct of the promisor himself","Conduct of any other person","Any act event or accident which is not in the control of the parties Le. Act of God","Either a or b"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Contract of Indemnity specifies that the Indemnity holder'),
(35, 'Bailment is an act of deliveringÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦?', '["Goods","Services","Goods and Services","None of the above."]'::jsonb, 0, 'According to Section 148 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, a " Bailment'''),
(36, 'What is the form of contract under the contract of guarantee?', '["Must be oral","Must be written","Either oral or written","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A contract'),
(37, 'Bhavan owes Chetan a debt guaranteed by Amit. Chetan does not sue Bhavan for a year after the debt has become payable. In the meantime, Bhuvan becomes insolvent. Is Amit discharged? Decide with reference to the provision of the Indian Contract Act, 1872?', '["Amit is discharged from his liability","Amit is discharged for half of the amount of Bhaven''s liability","Amit Is not discharged","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Amit is not discharged, since mere preference on the part of the creditor to sue the principal debtor does not discharge the surety.'),
(38, 'What are the rights of indemnity holder u/s 125?', '["Right to recover damages","Right to recover cost of suit","Right to recover the sums paid as a compromise of the suit","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'According to Section 125 of The Indian Contract Act, 1872, it defines the Rights of Indemnity Holder when sued, It pertaining to various conditions what kind of losses will be borne by Indemnifier who is been sued by the indemnity holder.'),
(39, 'Which among the following are not to be considered as a contract of indemnity?', '["A contract of Fire Insurance","A contract of Marine Insurance","A contract of Life Insurance","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A contract of Life Insurance is not covered un der the contract of indemnity as any act, event or accident which is not In the control of the parties/the act of God is not covered u/s 124.'),
(40, 'A contracts to save B against the consequences of any proceedings, which C may take against B in respect of a certain sum of 500 rupees. This is a:', '["Contract of","Quasi contract","Contract of indemnity","Void guarantee contract"]'::jsonb, 1, NULL),
(41, 'Ravi becomes guarantor for Ashok for the amount which may be given to him by Navin within 6 months. The maximum limit of the said amount is Rs. 1 Lakh. After two months Ravi withdraws his guarantee. Up to the time of revocation of guarantee. Navin had given to Ashok Rs. 20,000. Whether Ravi is discharged from his liabilities to Navin for any subsequent loan? And, whether Ravi is liable if Ashok fails to pay the amount of Rs. 20,000 to Navin?', '["Ravi is discharged from all the liabilities","Ravi is liable to pay the said maximum limit of Rs. 1, 00,000","Ravi is liable to pay the part of the amount of the maximum limit.","Ravi is not discharged for the loan of Rs. 20,000 already given by Navin to Ashok and discharged from all the liabilities after the date of revocation of continuing guarantee"]'::jsonb, 3, 'As per Section 130, continuing guarantee may be revoked, at any time, by the surety by giving a notice to the creditor. However, revocation shall be effective only In respect of future transaction (i.e. the liability of the surety with regard to previous transactions remains unaffected).'),
(42, 'C, the holder of an overdue bill of exchange drawn by A as surety for B, and accepted by B, contracts with X to give time to B. Is A discharged from his liability?', '["A is not discharged from his liability","A is discharged from his liability","A Is discharged from his liability to X","A Is discharged from his liability only to C"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A is not discharged from his liability, since, where a contract to give time to the principal debtor is made by the creditor with a third person and not with the principal debtor, the surety is not discharged.'),
(43, 'Where ''X'' obtains housing loan from LIC Housing and if ''Y'' promises to pay LIC Housing in the event of ''X'' failing to repay - Depict who is the Surety, the Principal Debtor and the Creditor in the given case?', '["X: Principal Debtor; LIC: Surety; Y: Creditor","X: Surety; LIC: Principal Debtor; Y: Creditor","X: Principal Debtor; LIC: Creditor. Y: Surety","X: Surety; LIC: Creditor; Y: Principal Debtor"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Hence In the above case, according to Section 126, Surety is the person who gives the guarantee; Principal Debtor: is the person In respect of whose default the guarantee Is given; Creditor: Is the person to whom the guarantee is given.'),
(44, 'In a Contract of Guarantee there is/are:', '["One contract","Two contracts","Three contracts","Four contracts"]'::jsonb, 2, NULL),
(45, 'A guarantee obtained by a creditor by keeping silence as to material circumstances is:', '["Valid","Voidable","Unenforceable","Invalid"]'::jsonb, 1, NULL),
(46, 'What is the meaning of "Indemnity"?', '["To make good the loss incurred by another person.","To compensate the party who has suffered some loss","To save a party from incurring a loss","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'According to Section 124 of the Indian Contract Act, 1872. A contract is called a "Contract of Indemnity" If one party promises to save the other from loss caused to him by the conduct of the promisor himself, or by the conduct of any other person.'),
(47, 'When the commencement of surety''s liability arises?', '["When the creditor has sued the principal debtor","On the default by the principal debtor","When the Creditor gives notice to the principal debtor for his default","When the principal debtor gives notice to the surety for such default"]'::jsonb, 1, 'As per Section 128, the liability of surety arises immediately on default by th e principal debtor.'),
(48, 'A gives to C a continuing guarantee to the extent of Rs. 5,000 for the vegetables to be supplied by C to B from time to time on credit. Afterwards, B became embarrassed and without the knowledge of A, B & C contract that C shall continue to supply B with vegetables for ready money, and that the payment shall be applied to both the then existing debt between B & C. Examining the provision of the Indian Contract Act, 1872, decide whether A is liable on his guarantee given to C?', '["Yes, A is liable","No, A is not liable, that take place variation between B & C","No, A is not liable for any of the payment made as surety to C","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A is not liable to C for the transaction that take place after variation between B & C, since a surety Is discharged If any transaction is made In a contract of guarantee without the consent of the surety.'),
(49, 'A asks H to kill Z and promises to indemnify H against the consequences. H kills Z and is fined Rs.1000. Can H (indemnity holder) is able to claim the amount of Rs. 1000 from A (indemnifier)?', '["Yes","No","Partly Yes & Partly No.","CanÃ¢â‚¬â„¢t say"]'::jsonb, 1, 'No, since the object of the agreement was unlawful as all the essentials of a valid contract must also be present in the contract of indemnity.'),
(50, 'What is the meaning of Contract of Guarantee u/s 126?', '["It is a contract to discharge petty liabilities of a third person in case of his default","It Is a contract to perform the promise of discharging the liabilities to any damages of o third person In case of his default","It is a contract to perform the promise or discharge the liability of a third person in case of his default","None of the above."]'::jsonb, 2, 'According to Section 126 of The Indian Contract Act, 1872, it defines the meaning of "Contract of Guarantee'', where a person himself performs the promise given or accepts to discharge the liability of a third person in case of his default.')
) as v(question_no, question, options, correct_index, hint)
where b.title = 'CA Inter Law MCQs by Darshan Khare'
on conflict (book_id, chapter_no, question_no) do nothing;


-- ==================== CHAPTER 12 ====================

-- Chapter 12: Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881
insert into public.mcq_questions (book_id, chapter_no, chapter_title, question_no, question, options, correct_index, hint)
select b.id, 12, 'Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881', v.question_no, v.question, v.options, v.correct_index, v.hint
from public.books b
cross join lateral (values
(1, 'A person signed a blank acceptance and kept it in his drawer and some person stole it and filled it up for Rs. 29,000 and negotiated it to an innocent person for value, In this case whether signer of the blank acceptance liable to the holder in due course?', '["it was held that the signer to the blank acceptance was liable to the holder in due course","It was held that the signer to the blank acceptance was liable to the holder in due course based on situations","it was held that the signer to the blank acceptance was not liable to the holder in due course","it was held that the signer to the blank acceptance was not liable to the holder in due course based on situations"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Example, a person signed a blank acceptance and kept It in his drawer and some person stole it and fined it up for Rs. 29,000 and negotiated it to an innocent person for value, it was held that the signer to the blank acceptance was not liable to the holde r in due course because he never delivered the instrument intending it to be used as a negotiable instrument. Further, as a condition of liability, the signer as a maker, drawer, endorser or acceptor must deliver the instrument to another. In the absence o f delivery, the signer Is not liable. Furthermore, the paper so signed and delivered must be stamped in accordance with the law prevalent at the time of signing and on delivering otherwise the signer is not estopped from showing that the instrument was fil led without his authority.'),
(2, 'Suraj draws a cheque In favour of Shekhar, a minor. Shekhar endorses the same in favour of Anural. The cheque Is dishonored by the bank on grounds of inadequate funds. Who is the person liable to pay here and Can Anural Sue Shekhar?', '["Person liable to Pay- Suraj, Anural Cannot sue Shekhar","Person liable to Pay- Shekhar, Anural Can sue Shekhar","Person liable to Pay- SuraJ, Anural Can sue Shekhar","No one is liable here"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Capacity to make, draw, accepts etc. of Instruments (Section 26): A minor may draw, endorse, deliver and negotiate such Instruments so as to bind all parties except himself. Nothing herein contained shall be deemed to empower a corporation to make, endorse or accept such instruments except in cases in which, under the law for the time being in force, they are so empowered. Example: Suraj draws a cheque in favour of Shekhar, a minor. Shekhar endorses the same in favour of Anural. The cheque is dishonored by the bank on grounds of Inadequate funds. Here in this case, Shekhar being a minor may draw, endorse, deliver and negotiate the instrument so as to bind all parties except himself. Therefore, Shekhar is not liable. Anural can, thus, proceed against Suraj.'),
(3, 'The main objective of the Negotiable instrument Act, 1881 is toÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.by which Instruments contemplated by it could pass from hand to hand by negotiation like any other goods.', '["Legalize the system","Penalize the person who fails to follow the system","To advise about process of transfer of negotiable instrument","to provide rules and regulation about validity of the instruments"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The source of Indian law relating to such instruments is admittedly the English Common Law. The main objective of the Act is to legalize the system by which instruments contemplat ed by It could pass from hand to hand by negotiation like any othe r goods.'),
(4, 'The Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 applies to whole of India but excludesÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.', '["Any State Government Transaction","Any local authority Transaction","Any Central Govt. Transactions","Transaction as per Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934, section 21 which provides the Bank to have the right to transact Government business In India"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The Act applies to the whole of India, but nothing herein contained affects the Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934, Section 21 which provides the Bank to have the right to transact Government business In India, or affects any local usage re lating to any instrument in an oriental language.'),
(5, 'The property in a negotiable instrument passes to aÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦..transferee for value.', '["Mala fide","Bona fide","Both a and b","Can be Mala fide as well as bona fide"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Negotiable Instruments is an instrument which is transferable (by customs of trade) by delivery, like cash, and is also capable of being sued upon by the person holding it for the time being. The property in such an instrument passes to a bona fide transferee for value.'),
(6, 'A promissory note must containÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.', '["Undertaking","Conditional undertaking","Unconditional undertaking","can be Conditional undertaking or Unconditional undertaking"]'::jsonb, 2, 'According to section 4 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 "A "promissory note" is an instrument In writing (not being a bank - note or a currency - note) containing an unconditional undertaking signed by the maker, to pay a certain sum of money only to, o r to the order of, a certain person, or to the bearer of the'),
(7, 'Where no rate of interest is specified in the bills of exchange or promissory notes then what is the interest rate used for calculating the interest amount?', '["15% P.A.","18% P.A.","12% P.A.","16% P.A."]'::jsonb, 1, 'When no rate of interest Is specified In the Instrument Ã¢â‚¬â€ interest on the amount due thereon shall be calculated at the rate of 18% per annum, from the date at which the same ought to have been paid by the party charged, until tender or realization of the amount due thereon, or until such date after the institution of a suit to recover such amount as the court directs.'),
(8, 'Who is the person so entitled at the time of such loss or destruction to receive or recover the amount due thereon from the parties where the note, bill or cheque is lost or destroyed?', '["acceptor","Drawer","Payer","holder"]'::jsonb, 3, '"Holder [Section 8] Ã¢â‚¬â€ The "holder of a promissory note, bill of exchange or cheque means Ã¢â‚¬â€ any person - entitled in his own name to the possession thereof, and - to receive or recover the amount due thereon from the parties thereto. Where the note, bill or ch eque is lost or destroyed, its holder is the person so entitled at the time of a. 2 b. 3 c. 9 d. 7'),
(9, 'If the holder of a bill of exchange allows the drawer more thanÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦..hours, exclusive of public holidays to consider whether he will accept the same, all previous parties not consenting to such allowance are thereby discharged from liability to such holder', '["36","24","72","48"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Discharge by allowing drawer more than forty - eight hours to accept [Section 83]: - If the holder of a bill of exchange allows the drawer more than forty eight hours, exclusive of public holidays, to consider whether he will accept the same, all previous parties not consenting to such allowance are thereby discharged from liability to such holder.'),
(10, 'In the case of any conviction in a summary trial, it shall be lawful for the Magistrate to pass a sentence of imprisonment for a term how many year?', '["not exceeding three year","not exceeding two year","not exceeding one year","not exceeding four year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Power of Court to try cases summarily [Sect ion 143]: - In case of summary trial : Provided that in the case of any conviction in a summary trial under this section, it shall be lawful for the Magistrate to pass a sentence of imprisonment for a term not exceeding one year and an amount of fine exceed ing five thousand rupees.'),
(11, 'A promissory note, bill of exchange or chequeÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.', '["may contain words prohibiting transfer or indicating an intention that it shall be transferable","does not contain words prohibiting transfer or Indicating an Intention that It shall not be transferable","does contain words prohibiting transfer or indicating an intention that it shall not be transferable","does not contain words prohibiting transfer or indicating an Intention that It shall be transferable"]'::jsonb, 1, 'A promissory note, bill of exchange or cheque is payable order which is expressed to be so payable or which is expressed to be payable to a particular person, and does not contain words prohibiting transfer or indicating an intention that it shall not be transferable.'),
(12, 'Cheque Is also a bill of exchange but is drawn on a banker andÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.', '["payable on demand","Payable after a certain period","at a designated future date or on demand","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A combined reading of Section 5 and 6 tells us that a bill of exchange is a negotiable instrument in writing containing an Instruction to a third party to pay a stated sum of money at a designated future date or on demand. Whereas, a cheque is also a bill of exchange but is drawn on a banker and payable on demand.'),
(13, 'Out of the below situation, Where it is called as Instruments payable on demand?', '["promissory note or bill of exchange, in which no time for payment Is specified","A promissory note or bill of exchange, in which Fixed time for payment is specified","A promissory note or bill of exchange, In which specified time for payment is specified to specified person","A promissory note or bill of exchange, in which no time for payment Is specified or fixed time Is specified"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Instruments payable on demand (Section 19) A promissory note or bill of exchange, in which no time for payment is specified, and a cheque, are payable on demand.'),
(14, 'Suraj draws a cheque for Rs.19, 000 and hands it over to Shekhar by way of gift on his marriage, Who is the holder in due course in this case?', '["Suraj","Shekhar","Both a and b","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'SuraJ draws a cheque for Rs.19, 000 and hands It over to Shekhar by way of gift on his marriage. Shekhar is a holder but not a holder in due course as he does not get the cheque for value and consideration. Hi s title is good and bonafide. As a holder he is entitled to receive Rs.19, 000 from the bank on whom the cheque is drawn'),
(15, 'When the day on which a promissory note or bill of exchange Is at maturity is a public holiday, the instrument shall be deemed to be due on theÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦..', '["next preceding business day","Previous business day","On the same date","Any of the above mentioned date"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When day of maturity is a holiday [Section 25] When the day on which a promissory note or bill of exchange is at maturity is a public holiday, the instrument shall be deemed to be due on the next preceding business day. Explanation: The expression "Public Holiday'' Includes Sundays and any other day declared by the Central Government, by notification in the Official Gazette, to b e a public holiday.'),
(16, 'Where a bill of exchange has been lost before it is overdue, the person who was the holder of it may apply to whom to give him another bill of the same tenor?', '["Drawer","acceptor","Endorser","Payer"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Holder''s right to duplicate of lost bill (Section 454) Where a bill of exchange has been lost before it is overdue, - the person who was the holder of it may apply - to the drawer to give him another bill of the same tenor, - giving security to the drawer, if required.'),
(17, 'Suraj executed a promissory note in favour of Shekhar for Rs. 50 lakhs. The said amount was payable three days after sight. Bhushan, on maturity, presented the promissory note on 1st March, 2017 to Bharat. Bharat made the payments on 4th January, 2017. What is the maturity date of the promissory note and whether Shekhar can recover interest for delay payment?', '["Maturity Date 3rd March 2017, Yes Can recover Interest","Maturity Date 3rd March 2017, No Cannot recover Interest","Maturity Date 4th March 2017, Yes Can recover Interest","Maturity Date 4th March 2017, No Cannot recover Interest"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In calculating the date at which a promissory note or bill of exchange made payable at certain number of days after date or after sight or after a certain event is at maturity, the day of the date, or of presentment for acceptance or sight, or of protest f or non - acceptance, or on which the event happens, shall be excluded. Section 24 of the Negotiable Instruments Act 1881 states that where a bill or note is payable after date or after sight or after happening of a specified event, the time of payment is'),
(18, 'When the maker or holder of a negotiable Instrument signs the same (otherwise than as such maker) for the purpose of negotiation is called as whom?', '["Acceptor","Drawer","Endorser","holder"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Endorsement [Section 15]: When the maker or holder of a negotiable instrument signs the same (otherwise than as such maker) Ã¢â‚¬â€ for the purpose of negotiation - on the back or face thereof or on a slip of paper annexed thereto, or so signs for the same purpose a'),
(19, 'A bill of exchange is a negotiable instalment in writing containing an instruction to a third party to pay a stated sum of moneyÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦..', '["Payable after a certain period","payable on demand at any point of time","at a designated future date or on demand","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, 'A combined reading of section 5 and 6 tells us that a bill of exchange is a negotiable instrument in writing containing an instruction to a third party to pay a stated sum of money at a designated'),
(20, 'As per section 6 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 Cheque in the electronic form means a cheque drawn in electronic form by using any computer resource, and signed in a secure system with a digital signatureÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦..', '["without biometric signature","with or without biometric signature","with biometric signature","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Cheque [Section 6]: - A cheq ue is a bill of exchange drawn on a specified banker and not expressed to be payable otherwise than on demand and it includes the electronic image of a truncated cheque and a cheque in the electronic form.'),
(21, 'After the drawee of a bill has signed his assent upon the bill, or, if there are more parts thereof than one, upon one of such pans, and delivered the same, or given notice of such signing to the holder or to some person on his behalf, he is called theÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦', '["Payer in case of need","acceptor","drawer in case of need","drawee In case of need"]'::jsonb, 1, 'After the drawee of a bill has signed his assent upon the bill, or, If there are more parts thereof than one, upon one of such parts, and delivered the same, or gi ven notice of such signing to the holder or to some person on his behalf, he is called the "acceptorÃ¢â‚¬Â.'),
(22, 'The maker of a bill of exchange or cheque is called theÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦. person thereby directed to pay is called theÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦..', '["Payer and receiver","Drawee and drawer","Payee and receiver","Drawer and drawee"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The maker of a bill of exchange or cheque is called the "drawerÃ¢â‚¬Â. The person thereby directed to pay is called the "drawee".'),
(23, 'Validity period for the presentation of cheque in bank isÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.', '["3 Months","6 Months","1 Year","2 Years"]'::jsonb, 0, NULL),
(24, 'Any person who for consideration became the possessor of a promissory note, bill of exchange or cheque is called asÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.', '["Acceptor in due course","Drawer","Holder in due course","Payer"]'::jsonb, 2, '"Holder in due course"[Section 9] means any person - who for consideration - became the possessor of a promissory note, bill of exchange or cheque (if payable to bearer), or the payee or endorsee thereof, (If payable to order), - before the amount m entioned in it became payable, and - without having sufficient cause to believe that any defect existed In the title of the person from whom he derived his title.'),
(25, 'Maker of a bill of exchange is called asÃ¢â‚¬â€', '["Drawer","Drawee","Acceptor","Payee"]'::jsonb, 0, NULL),
(26, 'A promissory note is an instrument inÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.', '["Writing","Photocopy","Xerox","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 0, 'According to section 4 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 A "promissory note" is an instrument in writing (not being a bank - note or a currency - note) containing an unconditional undertaking signed by the maker, to pay a certain sum of money only to, or to the order of, a certain person, or to the bearer of the instrument.'),
(27, 'A promissory note Is an Instrument in writing containing an unconditional undertaking signed by the whom?', '["Anyone","Payer","Receiver","maker"]'::jsonb, 3, 'According to section 4 of THE NEGOTIABLE INSTRUMENTS ACT, 1881 "A "promissory note" is an instrument In writing (not being a bank - note or a currency - note) containing an unconditional undertaking signed by the maker, to pay a certain sum of money only to, or to the order of, a certain person, or to the bearer of the instrument."'),
(28, 'Offences committed under the Negotiable Instruments Act can beÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦..', '["Compoundable","Non compoundable","Non compoundable and non-bailable","Bailable"]'::jsonb, 0, NULL),
(29, 'When in the bill the name of any person is given in addition to the drawee to be resorted to In case of need such person is calledÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.', '["Payer in case of need","Payee in case of need","drawer In case of need","drawee In case of need"]'::jsonb, 3, 'When in the bill or in any endorsement thereon the name of any person Is given in addition to the drawee to be resorted to in case of need such person Is called a " drawee In case of need"'),
(30, 'As per Section 13 of the Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881 A "negotiable Instrument'' means a promissory note, bill of exchange or cheque payable to whom?', '["order or to payer","order or to receiver","order or to bearer","order or to withdrawer"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Section 13 says, A "negotiable instrument" means a promissory note, bill of exchange or cheque payable either to order or to bearer.')
) as v(question_no, question, options, correct_index, hint)
where b.title = 'CA Inter Law MCQs by Darshan Khare'
on conflict (book_id, chapter_no, question_no) do nothing;


-- ==================== CHAPTER 13 ====================

-- Chapter 13: The General Clauses Act, 1897
insert into public.mcq_questions (book_id, chapter_no, chapter_title, question_no, question, options, correct_index, hint)
select b.id, 13, 'The General Clauses Act, 1897', v.question_no, v.question, v.options, v.correct_index, v.hint
from public.books b
cross join lateral (values
(1, 'A wordÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦raise a presumption of something which Is not mandatory.', '["Shall","May","Could","Can"]'::jsonb, 1, NULL),
(2, 'Where legislation has not specifically mentioned date to come into force on a prescribed date, it shall Implemented onÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦..', '["Day that receives the assent of prime minister","Day that receives the assent of president","Both of them","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, NULL),
(3, 'The preamble is most important in any legislation, itÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.', '["Provides definitions in the act","Expresses scope, object and purpose of act","Provides summary of entire act","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, NULL),
(4, 'Some definitions usedÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.such as definitions are exhaustive definitions and exactly define the term.', '["Imply","Include","Points","Means"]'::jsonb, 3, NULL),
(5, 'The actÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.defines any "Territorial extent- clause.', '["Mearley","Not","Fully","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, NULL),
(6, 'Official gazette means -', '["The gazette of India","The official gazette of the India","Both of them","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 1, NULL),
(7, 'Which of the following is not included in the definition of Immovable property u/s 3(26):', '["Machinery fixed to the soil","Buildings","Timber","Standing crops"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Timer Is not immovable property as the same are not permanently attached to the earth.'),
(8, 'Any matter written, expressed or described upon any substance by means of letters, figures or more than one means for purpose of recording is calledÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦..', '["Deed","Document","Agreement","Contract"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Document Is any matter written expressed or described upon any substance by means of letters, figures or more than one means for purpose of recording.'),
(9, 'Section shall mean aÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.', '["Section of act or regulation in which word occurs","Schedule to act and or regulation in which word occurs","Both of them","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 0, NULL),
(10, 'Service by post shall be deemed to be effected when:', '["Properly addressing","Pre- paying","Posting by registered post","All of the above"]'::jsonb, 3, 'All the conditions are necessary.'),
(13, 'Government includes', '["Central government","State government","Both of them","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, NULL),
(16, 'Power to appoint includesÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.', '["Power to appoint ex-officio","Power to suspend or dismiss","Both of them","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, NULL),
(19, 'Some definitions such asÃ¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦Ã¢â‚¬Â¦.shows extensive definitions.', '["Include","Mean","Conclude","Point"]'::jsonb, 0, NULL),
(20, 'Which is not object of General clauses act?', '["To shorten the language the central act","To provide as far as possible for uniformity of expression in central act, by giving definitions of a series of terms In common use","To elaborate language act","None of the above"]'::jsonb, 2, NULL),
(21, 'Scope, object and purpose of the act Is expressed through:', '["Legislation","Preamble","Rules","Statute"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Every act has a preamble which expresses the scope, object and purpose of the act.'),
